False alarms represented 96.5% of all alarm calls and accounted for nearly 400 hours of
police service at a minimum expense of $20,000 to the taxpayers.  The time spent
responding to false alarms may cause an extended response to a real emergency.

The current ordinance does not require call verification by the alarm company prior to calling
the police.  Thus, the Cheyenne Police Department began a response to 352 calls in 2012
before being cancelled.  Currently, alarm companies call the police then call the alarm
holder to confirm the need for the police to respond.  Call verification would require the
alarm company to call the alarm holder before calling the police; thus, eliminating 352 police
calls for service.

Currently, there is no coordination and verification of alarm permits with police calls.
Therefore, the police respond to alarms that may not be licensed in the City of Cheyenne.
The City has 1,026 alarm permits on file.  An evaluation of other cities of similar size
revealed that Cheyenne may very well have in excess of 2,000 alarms.  Important alarm
holder information, which the police may need if an actual crime was discovered, is not
accessible to the police for those alarms that are not licensed.

Currently, the City collects an annual $25 permit fee from each alarm holder, which totals
approximately $25,000 a year.  The alarm holder information is then entered into three
separate computer systems by three separate employees.   A majority of the information
does not change from year to year; however, it takes valuable time to reenter the
information year after year.
```

### Page 3 of 6

```text
 Page 3
Under the current ordinance the City may charge a fee of $100 for the sixth false alarm
within in year.  An evaluation of the 2012 false alarms revealed that the City could have
collected $25,000 in false alarm fees.  Since, there is no consistent coordination or
accountability in collection, the City collects on average $4,000 to $12,000 a year in false
alarm fees.

There is no provision in the current ordinance to suspend a permit when the alarm is
deemed to be unreliable.  Thus, the police continue to respond to undependable alarms at
great expense to the City.  In addition, officers may become complacent when repeatedly
responding to the same undependable alarm, which can have deadly results if an actual
crime was in progress.

Discussion:

The National Burglary and Fire Alarm Association and False Alarm Reduction Association
recommend ordinances as the one being proposed.  The alarm industry strives to produce
reliable service; thus, they support graduated fees for false alarms, call verification and no
response to alarms deemed as unreliable.  These practices are also supported by the
International Association of Chiefs of Police.

The proposed revisions are as follows:

8.08.020
 Lifetime permit
 A majority of permit holders are responsible and do not cause any police
resources to be wasted.  Thus, these people would be rewarded by not being
required to renew their permit on an annual basis, which takes staff time to
update City records.
 Requirement to update alarm holder information with a minimum of two phone
numbers for police emergency contact.

8.08.025
 Those who currently have a permit will receive the lifetime permit for the current
renew fee of $25.

8.08.030
 The Chief of Police or his designee may revoke a permit with proper notice for
nonpayment of fees or excessive false alarms.
 The Police Department would appoint an alarm administrator, whose primary
job will be to work with the alarm holders to reduce false alarms.  Permit
revocation will be the last resort and only when the permittee fails to take any
action to prevent future false alarms.
```

### Page 4 of 6

```text
 Page 4
 The permittee may appeal to a city council panel as appointed by the Council
President.
 The permit may be reinstated at any time when the permittee displays an
attempt to solve the problem.
 If a permit is revoked, the police will not respond (after proper notice) to that
address for unreliable alarm activation unless there is a verified crime, since
the alarm has been proven to be defective.  The police will always respond to
a panic or hold-up alarms.

8.08.040
 The following graduated fees may be implemented for false alarms within the
calendar year:
 1st response – no action
 2nd response – The alarm administrator will mail a letter to the alarm holder, to
request a written response as to what was done to prevent future false alarms
and to warn that a 3rd response will result in a fee.
 3rd response - $100 fee
 The alarm holder may complete online alarm training to avoid the fee.
 4th response - $200 fee
 The Cheyenne Police Citizens on Patrol will attempt to make contact
with the alarm holder to offer in person alarm training.
 5th response - $400 fee
 6th and subsequent response - $400 fee or revocation of permit
 The primary goal is to reduce false alarms and not to collect revenue.  A fee
may not be applied to alarm holders who show that they have taken steps to
prevent false alarms.

8.08.055
 Call verification
 The alarm monitoring company shall attempt to reach the alarm holder prior
to calling the police unless it is a panic or hold-up alarm.
 Alarm companies shall train alarm holders how to use the alarm and advise them of
the permit requirement.

The Cheyenne Police Department recently updated its computer system to track the
provisions of this ordinance.  Upon responding to an alarm call the alarm administrator will
insure that the alarm holder has a permit.  Assessed fees will be forwarded to the City
Treasurer for collection and recording.  Additionally, the computer revision has reduced the
number of employees to complete data entry from three down to two.
```

### Page 5 of 6

```text
 Page 5
Fiscal Considerations:

If the false alarm fees as proposed in this ordinance were applied to the 2012 calls for
service to following would have been collected:

189 alarms with 3 responses x $100   =  $  18,900
114 alarms with 4 responses x $200   =  $  22,800
79   alarms with 5 responses x $400   =
$  31,600
All other responses above 5  x $400   =   $105,600 (Assuming no revocations occurred)
False alarm fee total

$178,900

However, the purpose of this ordinance is to reduce false alarms and improve police
efficiency.  Thus, it is estimated that approximately $50,000 in fees will be collected in the
first year and this number will decrease each additional year the ordinance is in effect.

In addition, supplementary fines and permit fees would be collected for those alarm holders
who do not have permits.

Other Considerations:

The proposed ordinance will have little effect on Cheyenne homeowners since a majority of
the problems occur with businesses, as the following 2012 data reflects:

Only 16 residences had four or more false alarms; however, they accounted for 77 calls for
service.

Seventy-seven businesses had four or more false alarms and accounted for 589 calls for
service.

Five schools required four or more alarm responses, accounting for 55 calls for service.

Twelve government buildings accounted for 82 calls for service and five churches required
34 alarm calls for service.

Very few alarm holders cause a majority of the false alarm problems for the Cheyenne
Police Department.  If this ordinance is successful in eliminating four or more calls for
service by an alarm holder it would eliminate 800 calls a year.

Recommendation:

In 2012 the Cheyenne Police Department responded to alarms at an average of 310 per
10,000 residents.  Other cities that have implemented call verification, false alarm fees and
permit revocation have seen over a 50% reduction in calls.  For example; Boulder, CO
```

### Page 6 of 6

```text
 Page 6
averages 140 calls per 10,000 residents, Fremont, CA averages 55 calls per 10,000
residents, and Lakewood, CO averages 125 calls per 10,000 residents.

The Cheyenne Police Department cannot continue its current level of service with an
increasing demand on resources.  It has to become efficient in providing services where
they are needed.  Thus, staff recommends the passage of the ordinance without any
recommendation on the fees that should be applied within the ordinance.
```
